<section id="product"><div class="sh"><span class="no">01</span><h2>The product</h2></div>
<p class="lede">A deck of dua cards you can browse the way you actually feel: by situation, by need, by set.
Each card carries the Arabic, how to say it, what it means, where it comes from, and when it may be read.</p>
<div class="grid2">
 <div class="rule"><b>The shape</b><p>A card deck, not a scripture reader. You pick up a card, turn it over, and put it down. Sets group cards by life context; categories cut across sets.</p></div>
 <div class="rule"><b>The promise</b><p>Every authentic dua and every essential tool is free, forever. Premium supports the app and unlocks learning, personalisation, journeys, audio and storage.</p></div>
 <div class="rule"><b>The line</b><p>Never “unlock more duas”. The paid tier is described as supporting the app and unlocking advanced tools. The religious content is never the paywall.</p></div>
 <div class="rule"><b>Language</b><p>Bilingual and live. The EN / BM toggle in the masthead switches every card title, purpose, meaning, reading and source to the Malay layer already held in the data.</p></div>
</div></section>

<section id="deck"><div class="sh"><span class="no">02</span><h2>The deck</h2></div>
<p class="lede">Three axes. A card belongs to exactly one <b>set</b>, which decides its theme and its code.
It is of one <b>kind</b>, dua or surah, which says what sort of text it carries. And it carries one or more <b>categories</b>, which say what it is about and cut across every set. Kind and category are filed and filtered the same way, and each gets its own token on the card.</p>
<h3>Sets</h3>
<table class="t"><thead><tr><th>Set</th><th>Code</th><th>Theme</th><th>Cards</th><th>Covers</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class="sw" style="background:#5B8CFF"></span><b>Doa Gen Z Set 1</b></td><td class="mono">GZ</td><td>Indigo</td><td class="num">12</td><td class="desc">Pressure, doubt, focus and the inner life of a young Muslim.</td></tr><tr><td><span class="sw" style="background:#F06292"></span><b>Doa Travel Set 1</b></td><td class="mono">TR</td><td>Rose</td><td class="num">9</td><td class="desc">Departure, the road, arrival, and the family left behind.</td></tr><tr><td><span class="sw" style="background:#E8B54A"></span><b>Doa Rezeki Set 1</b></td><td class="mono">RZ</td><td>Gold</td><td class="num">4</td><td class="desc">Provision, acceptance of deeds, mercy and forgiveness.</td></tr><tr><td><span class="sw" style="background:#37C98F"></span><b>Doa Protection Set 1</b></td><td class="mono">PR</td><td>Emerald</td><td class="num">5</td><td class="desc">Refuge, steadfast faith, and safety in this life and the next.</td></tr><tr><td><span class="sw" style="background:#D9B25A"></span><b>Surah Set 1</b></td><td class="mono">SR</td><td>Black &amp; gold</td><td class="num">5</td><td class="desc">Al-Fatihah and the four Quls: the surahs recited daily and in every prayer.</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h3>Categories</h3>
<p class="lede">Thirteen, after research into how Hisnul Muslim and the established azkar apps group duas. The five with no cards yet stay visible but dimmed, so the deck states what it is going to hold.</p>
