 <ol class="leg"><li><span class="ln">01</span><div><b>Title bar</b><p>The card name in Space Grotesk, and the numbered diamond pip. The number is the card’s position in its set, not the global index.</p></div></li><li><span class="ln">02</span><div><b>Picture</b><p>Until the artwork exists this window holds the card's category glyph on a patterned ground. The written art brief is production direction, so it appears only in dev mode (?dev=1) and never to a reader. Real art replaces the brief in the same box.</p></div></li><li><span class="ln">03</span><div><b>Bismillah</b><p>بِسْمِ اللَّهِ set along the bottom edge of the picture, the way a mushaf places it as a header above the text. Moving it here gives the dua the whole text box.</p></div></li><li><span class="ln">04</span><div><b>Type line</b><p>KIND · CATEGORY in caps: DUA or SURAH, then the first category. The card’s filing label, with the use line tucked directly under it.</p></div></li><li><span class="ln">05</span><div><b>Use line</b><p>One sentence: what this dua asks for. The reader should know whether this card is for them from this line alone.</p></div></li><li><span class="ln">06</span><div><b>The dua</b><p>Arabic in Scheherazade New, fully vocalised, canonical Uthmani. Scrolls when long; the reader controls the size. Waqf pause marks are tinted and name themselves on hover. This is the reason the card exists.</p></div></li><li><span class="ln">07</span><div><b>Transliteration</b><p>Latin reading line for readers still learning the script.</p></div></li><li><span class="ln">08</span><div><b>Meaning &amp; source</b><p>The English meaning, with the Qur’an reference in mono beneath it. Centred with the Arabic so the three read as one quoted block. The source never leaves the claim.</p></div></li><li><span class="ln">09</span><div><b>Foot</b><p>Set name and card number at the left, the kind and category tokens at the right, as bare coloured icons.</p></div></li></ol>
</div>
