</div>
<div class="note"><b>What the back deliberately does not have</b>
<p>A girih tessellation ground, arabesque corner spandrels, khatim strapwork and a double-rule frame were all built
and then removed. At 334px wide they turned to noise. One pattern, one glow, one mark. No text competes with the pattern: the reading lives on the front, one tap deep.</p></div>
<h3>Tajwid colours &middot; trial</h3>
<p class="lede">One card, Opening the Heart &amp; Easing Speech (Taha 20:25&ndash;28), colours its rule sites the way a
tajwid mushaf does. Detection reads the marks already present in the verified Uthmani string; nothing is retyped, and
izhar stays in ink, as the mushafs leave it. Each coloured site names its rule on hover. The key lives here, not on the
card: the card stays a reading, the Codex holds the legend. Machine-detected, awaiting scholar review before it spreads
to the rest of the deck.</p>
