<section id="model"><div class="sh"><span class="no">04</span><h2>Content model</h2></div>
<p class="lede">One flat record per card. Everything the front, the back and the spotlight need comes from these fields. Nothing about a card is computed from its position in the array except its number.</p>
<table class="t"><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Language</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="mono key">set</td><td class="mono dim">key</td><td class="desc">Which deck the card belongs to: genz · travel · rezeki · protection. Drives the theme.</td></tr><tr><td class="mono key">kind</td><td class="mono dim">key</td><td class="desc">What sort of text the card carries: <b>dua</b> or <b>surah</b>. Filed and filtered exactly like a category, and it gets the first token on both faces.</td></tr><tr><td class="mono key">ten</td><td class="mono dim">English</td><td class="desc">Card title. Short, dignified, no hype. Shown on both faces.</td></tr><tr><td class="mono key">pen</td><td class="mono dim">English</td><td class="desc">Use line: what this dua asks for, in one sentence. Sits under the type line on the front.</td></tr><tr><td class="mono key">ar</td><td class="mono dim">Arabic</td><td class="desc">The dua text itself. Canonical Uthmani, transcribed from an approved source, never authored. Waqf pause marks are part of this string.</td></tr><tr><td class="mono key">verified</td><td class="mono dim">ref</td><td class="desc">Which text edition the Arabic came from, e.g. Tanzil Uthmani (Hafs). Records that the string was checked, not just typed.</td></tr><tr><td class="mono key">tr</td><td class="mono dim">Latin</td><td class="desc">Transliteration for readers still learning the script.</td></tr><tr><td class="mono key">en</td><td class="mono dim">English</td><td class="desc">Translation, shown as the quoted line at the foot of the front face.</td></tr><tr><td class="mono key">srcEn</td><td class="mono dim">ref</td><td class="desc">Source in English form, e.g. Qur’an · Al-Furqan 25:74. Travels with every copy and share.</td></tr><tr><td class="mono key">art</td><td class="mono dim">English</td><td class="desc">The art brief: a written description of the image still to be made. Shown in the picture window in dev mode only; readers see the category glyph until real artwork replaces it.</td></tr><tr><td class="mono key">ken</td><td class="mono dim">English</td><td class="desc">When it may be read. Guidance without promising outcomes. Shown on the card back.</td></tr><tr><td class="mono key">aen</td><td class="mono dim">English</td><td class="desc">Context of revelation, where a reliable account exists. Shown on the card back.</td></tr><tr><td class="mono key">cats</td><td class="mono dim">keys</td><td class="desc">One or more category keys. The first drives the glyph in the picture and the stamp in the foot. Not every card needs more than one.</td></tr><tr><td class="mono key">t · p · my · keg · asb</td><td class="mono dim">Malay</td><td class="desc">The original Malay fields, carried unused until the BM toggle ships.</td></tr></tbody></table>
</section>

<section id="design"><div class="sh"><span class="no">05</span><h2>Design system</h2></div>
<p class="lede">Four themes over one variable grammar. Each set re-colours the same tokens; nothing else changes.</p>
<h3>The mark</h3>
<p class="lede" style="margin-bottom:18px"><b>Baik</b> is the logo. Baik means good, and well: the answer you want to be able to give
when someone asks how you are. Its letters are drawn, not typed (assets/logo), and the dot over the &#305; is a diamond, the same diamond that
numbers every card. The word is the mark; there is no separate symbol to remember.</p>
<div class="logogrid">
 <div class="logocell"><div class="lgbox"><span class="baik-logo" role="img" aria-label="Baik" style="color:#1E2124"></span></div><b>Primary</b><p>Takes its colour from the surface, so one lockup serves every set theme.</p></div>
 <div class="logocell"><div class="lgbox gold"><span class="baik-logo" role="img" aria-label="Baik" style="color:#C9961E"></span></div><b>Gold</b><p>The masthead, and anything on near-black.</p></div>
 <div class="logocell"><div class="lgbox sm"><span class="baik-logo" role="img" aria-label="Baik" style="color:#1E2124"></span></div><b>Small</b><p>Under 40px the diamond closes up. Keep the word, drop nothing else.</p></div>
</div>
<table class="t" style="margin-top:22px"><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><b>Clear space</b></td><td class="desc">One cap height on every side. On the card back the mark sits at the true vertical centre, and nothing crowds it.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>The diamond</b></td><td class="desc">It replaces the dot on the &#305;, sitting slightly low rather than centred. It is the same diamond as the card pip, which is what ties the mark to the deck.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Colour</b></td><td class="desc">Gold on dark, or the surface accent. Never two colours inside the word.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Never</b></td><td class="desc">Do not re-set the word in another face, restore the ordinary dot, add a crescent, or stretch it.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Why not a crescent</b></td><td class="desc">The star and crescent is an Ottoman and national emblem, not a religious one. Classical practice favours the word and the geometry, so the logo is a word.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>The tagline</b></td><td class="desc">&ldquo;The right dua for what you are facing.&rdquo; It says what the app does at the moment someone needs it, rather than describing the catalogue.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<h3>Typography</h3>
<table class="t"><thead><tr><th>Face</th><th>Used for</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Space Grotesk</b></td><td>The mark, and every card name</td><td class="desc">Carries the Methodas card lineage. The mark is set in it, so the logo and the deck share one voice.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Scheherazade New</b></td><td>The dua text, and the Bismillah</td><td class="desc">Built for fully vocalised Qur’anic text. It carries the harakat and the waqf marks correctly at size. Never swap this for a display face.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Reem Kufi &middot; Amiri</b></td><td>Arabic display, where display Arabic is needed</td><td class="desc">Kept loaded for headings and future surah titling. Neither is ever used for vocalised Qur’anic body text.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Saira</b></td><td>Body, purpose lines, translations</td><td class="desc">Reads calmly at small sizes.</td></tr><tr><td><b>JetBrains Mono</b></td><td>Sources, codes, labels</td><td class="desc">Every piece of metadata: card codes, references, stamps.</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h3>Tokens</h3>
<p class="lede" style="margin-bottom:16px">One builder draws every token on the card, so the two faces can never disagree about what a card is.
The kind comes first, then one token per category.</p>
<div class="grid2">
 <div class="rule"><b>Front &middot; flat</b><p>The bare coloured icon, no container. It sits on the card face like printed art rather than like a button, and it lives in the foot beside the set and number.</p></div>
 <div class="rule"><b>Back &middot; sphere</b><p>The same icon on a lit sphere: highlight top left, shadow bottom right. The glyph fills roughly two thirds of the sphere, which is what makes it readable at 30px.</p></div>
 <div class="rule"><b>Colour is the key</b><p>Every category owns one colour and keeps it everywhere: token, filter chip, foot stamp. Nothing else on the card competes for those hues.</p></div>
 <div class="rule"><b>Hover tells you</b><p>Every token, pause mark, source line and pip carries a tooltip with a title, an explanation and a kicker. Nothing on the card is a symbol you are expected to already know.</p></div>
</div>
<h3>Reading marks</h3>
<p class="lede" style="margin-bottom:16px">The waqf signs are part of the revealed text, not decoration. They are tinted gold so a reader
can see where to stop, and each one names itself on hover.</p>
<table class="t"><thead><tr><th>Sign</th><th>Name</th><th>What it instructs</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td style="font-family:Scheherazade New,serif;font-size:20px;color:#C9961E">&#1752;</td><td><b>Mim</b></td><td class="desc">Must stop. Continuing changes the meaning.</td></tr>
<tr><td style="font-family:Scheherazade New,serif;font-size:20px;color:#C9961E">&#1754;</td><td><b>Qali</b></td><td class="desc">Better to stop, though you may continue.</td></tr>
<tr><td style="font-family:Scheherazade New,serif;font-size:20px;color:#C9961E">&#1753;</td><td><b>Lam alif</b></td><td class="desc">Do not stop here. Carry on to the next pause.</td></tr>
<tr><td style="font-family:Scheherazade New,serif;font-size:20px;color:#C9961E">&#1755;</td><td><b>Three dots</b></td><td class="desc">Appears twice. Stop at one of the pair, not both.</td></tr>
<tr><td style="font-family:Scheherazade New,serif;font-size:20px;color:#C9961E">&#1750;</td><td><b>Sali</b></td><td class="desc">Better to continue, though you may stop.</td></tr>
<tr><td style="font-family:Scheherazade New,serif;font-size:20px;color:#C9961E">&#1751;</td><td><b>Jim</b></td><td class="desc">Stop or continue. Both are permitted.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<div class="note"><b>Tajweed colouring is deliberately not built</b>
<p>Colour-coding the rules of recitation would have to come from a verified annotation dataset keyed to this exact text.
Writing a rule engine by hand would risk teaching a wrong recitation, so the app ships the pause marks it can stand behind
and leaves the colouring until the data is in.</p></div>
<h3>Ornament</h3>
<div class="grid2">
 <div class="rule"><b>Star tile</b><p>An eight-point star repeating quietly across the card back, drawn as a mask so it takes each set&rsquo;s colour.</p></div>
 <div class="rule"><b>Hatching</b><p>Diagonal stripes mark the picture window as a placeholder in dev mode. A reader sees the category glyph on that ground instead, and both give way when real artwork lands.</p></div>
 <div class="rule"><b>The pip</b><p>A faceted diamond carrying the card number, top right of every front.</p></div>
 <div class="rule"><b>Glyphs</b><p>Each category and each kind owns one line glyph. The same drawing serves the flat front token, the back sphere and the picture watermark.</p></div>
</div></section>

