<section id="surfaces"><div class="sh"><span class="no">09</span><h2>Surfaces, account and typography</h2></div>
<p class="lede">What the portal grew into: three ways to hold the deck, an account layer with real payment shapes, and the typography decisions behind the Arabic.</p>

<h3 class="subh">Three views of one deck</h3>
<div class="grid2">
 <div class="rule"><b>Grid</b><p>The whole spread, grouped by set. The default, and the fastest way to scan what exists.</p></div>
 <div class="rule"><b>Deck</b><p>One card at a time on a stacked deck. Prev, next, arrow keys, swipe on touch, and <i>Draw for me</i> for a random card from whatever the filters leave in hand.</p></div>
 <div class="rule"><b>Table</b><p>A felt surface. <i>My table</i> lays out saved cards where you drag them, positions remembered. <i>Ask the table</i> takes problem cubes, money, marriage, health, anxious heart, grief, travel, study, protection, forgiveness, and deals the cards that score against the combination.</p></div>
 <div class="rule"><b>The finder</b><p>Search sits beside a tag panel: set and kind pick one, category ticks many, each with live counts. Chosen tags restate under the field as removable chips, so the filter state is never hidden behind a closed panel.</p></div>
</div>

<h3 class="subh">Categories · thirteen</h3>
<p>Eight shipped with the first sets. Five were added on research into how Hisnul Muslim and the established azkar apps group duas: the emotional register and the daily rhythm were missing entirely. Empty categories stay visible but dimmed, so the deck states what it will hold.</p>
<div class="grid2">
 <div class="rule"><b>Carrying cards</b><p>Rizq &amp; Work · Health &amp; Healing · Travel · Protection · Forgiveness &amp; Repentance · Home &amp; Family · Food &amp; Drink · Knowledge &amp; Study</p></div>
 <div class="rule"><b>Waiting on content</b><p>Marriage &amp; Love · Anxiety &amp; the Heart · Grief &amp; Loss · Morning &amp; Evening · Gratitude &amp; Praise</p></div>
</div>

<h3 class="subh">Account, payment and trial</h3>
<div class="grid2">
 <div class="rule"><b>Sign in and register</b><p>Google, Apple, or email with a six-digit code. No passwords anywhere. An account is never demanded to read: it is asked for at save, at purchase, at the trial.</p></div>
 <div class="rule"><b>What the account holds</b><p>Saved cards with private notes and named collections, recently opened, plan and renewal, transactions with reference numbers, language, data export, and a clear-everything button.</p></div>
 <div class="rule"><b>Checkout</b><p>FPX, card, Touch 'n Go, DuitNow QR, GrabPay, in the order a Malaysian expects. Monthly renews on card only and says so; annual and lifetime are one payments that take any method. Wallet and bank payments genuinely pass through <i>Pending</i> before <i>Paid</i>.</p></div>
 <div class="rule"><b>The trial</b><p>Seven days, nothing charged, the end date stated on the plan page, a reminder promised before it ends, and a Stop button the same size as Continue. Nothing renews by itself.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="note"><b>One price list</b><p>Monthly RM19.90 · Annual RM179.10 · Lifetime RM358.20 · Founding member RM129.90. Every surface reads from a single constant, so the sheet, the account and the plan table can never quote different numbers.</p></div>
<div class="note"><b>Premium is sold on what exists, not on the roadmap</b><p>Only unlimited saves, collections and private notes work today. Recitation audio, guided journeys, word-by-word meaning and offline packs are marked <i>in build</i> wherever they appear, in the app sheet and on the site, with a plain sentence saying so. The trial is free precisely so someone can see what exists before anything is charged.</p></div>
<div class="note"><b>Payment is never framed as worship</b><p>Premium supports the app's development. It is never presented as sadaqah, zakat or supporting Islam, and no dua is ever behind it. Giving exists separately and unlocks nothing.</p></div>

<h3 class="subh">Typography of the Arabic</h3>
<div class="grid2">
 <div class="rule"><b>The script</b><p>Scheherazade New (SIL) by default, for its mark-to-mark handling and full Quranic mark coverage. Amiri Quran (OFL) is offered as a second script, closer to a mushaf's colour. KFGQPC Uthmanic Hafs v2 carries the true Madinah letterforms and is the upgrade path, but it needs a self-hosted binary whose licence must be cleared first.</p></div>
 <div class="rule"><b>Size and leading</b><p>26, 30 or 35px, line-height 2.15 for Scheherazade and 2.35 for Amiri Quran. Stacked harakat need 2.0 at minimum; below that the marks clip the line above.</p></div>
 <div class="rule"><b>Gold on the waqf marks only</b><p>The pause marks are tinted so a reader can see where to stop, and each explains itself on hover. Nothing else in the ayah is coloured. Full tajweed colouring is permissible but teachers warn it becomes a crutch, and it would demand a glyph font with rules baked in.</p></div>
 <div class="rule"><b>Never touch the shaping</b><p>No letter-spacing, no synthetic bold, no splitting a word across elements, no mixing fonts mid-verse. In motion the Arabic moves as one block; it is never revealed letter by letter.</p></div>
</div>

<h3 class="subh">Where text comes from</h3>
<p>No Arabic is ever typed, pasted from a webpage, or generated. Qur'anic text comes from Tanzil or the QUL exports keyed by surah and ayah; hadith duas come from sunnah.com keyed by collection and number, with the grading carried through. The English and Malay prose around a dua is ours to write. The dua itself never is.</p>
<p>Every card therefore stores the key it was fetched with, so its text can be regenerated and audited rather than trusted:</p>
<table class="t"><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td class="mono">srcKind</td><td>Qur'an or hadith</td><td class="mono dim">quran</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">srcKey</td><td>The canonical key the text was pulled with</td><td class="mono dim">25:74 · bukhari:6389</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">srcText</td><td>Which edition the characters came from</td><td class="mono dim">tanzil-uthmani-hafs</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">srcFetched</td><td>When it was pulled</td><td class="mono dim">2026-07-26</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">verified</td><td>What confirmed it</td><td class="mono dim">Tanzil Uthmani (Hafs)</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">grading</td><td>Hadith only</td><td class="mono dim">sahih</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">review</td><td>Scholar review state and date</td><td class="mono dim">pending</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<div class="note"><b>The test for the pipeline</b><p>Re-running the fetch script must reproduce the same Arabic, byte for byte. Any card missing a source key, its text, or its reference is refused rather than shipped with a gap.</p></div>

<h3 class="subh">Sharing</h3>
<p>Any card renders to a 1080 by 1620 image drawn on canvas: the set colour, the title, the Arabic as one block, the transliteration, the meaning, and the source line with the Baik mark burned into the picture. A shared card can always be checked, because the reference cannot be cropped off a caption.</p>
</section>

<section id="state"><div class="sh"><span class="no">10</span><h2>Build state</h2></div>
<p class="lede">Where the web build stands, and what the app port will need.</p>
<div class="grid2">
 <div class="rule"><b>Shipping now</b><p>35 cards across 5 sets, all with canonical Uthmani text and waqf pause marks. Three views: grid, deck and table. The finder with tick-tags and removable chips. Bahasa Melayu live across every card and label. Flip, spotlight, About-this-dua on the card front, daily dua, saves with notes and collections, recent, copy and share with source attached, share as image, Arabic size and script, tooltips on every token, accounts with passwordless sign-in, checkout with Malaysian methods, a seven day trial, and email capture for the morning dua.</p></div>
 <div class="rule"><b>Next content pass</b><p>The five empty categories, Morning &amp; Evening first as the highest-traffic set, then Grief &amp; Loss and the forty Rabbanas. All of it through the verified pipeline, then a named scholar review with dates against every card.</p></div>
 <div class="rule"><b>Next build pass</b><p>A real backend: accounts, the lead list, the payment gateway, and the renewal reminders that are currently only promised in copy. Then audio, the largest single lift and the strongest premium argument.</p></div>
 <div class="rule"><b>Not started</b><p>Tajweed colouring, which is waiting on verified data and may stay out by choice. Cloud sync, a real gateway behind the checkout shapes, Qibla and prayer times, memorisation modes, offline packs, and the guided Hajj and Umrah journeys.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="note"><b>The app toggle is a layout preview, not a build</b><p>The App view drops the same deck into a phone-width frame so the layout can be judged at shipping size. A real mobile app is a separate port: the card, its data and its logic move across as they are, but the shell, navigation, storage and payments are rebuilt in whichever runtime is chosen.</p></div><div class="note"><b>One technical debt worth naming</b>
<p>The app is a single self-contained HTML file, which makes it trivial to copy and host, but the fonts load from
Google. True offline needs them bundled. That is a small change and should happen before any offline claim ships.</p></div>
</section>

